24.04_VB_LinuxVMImages.COM.vdi.

That file is a Virtual Disk Image (.vdi), which means it is a pre-installed virtual hard drive, not an installation CD (.iso). You do not need to run the Ubuntu installation process; you just need to attach this virtual hard drive to your VM.

Since you already have a VM definition created, you need to swap out its blank hard drive for this .vdi file.

1
Open Storage Settings
Select your VM in the VirtualBox Manager, click the Settings gear icon, and select Storage from the left menu.

2
Remove the Blank Drive
Under Controller: SATA (or IDE), find the existing .vdi attachment (usually named after your VM). Right-click it and select Remove Attachment.

3
Add the Existing Disk
Click the Add Hard Disk icon (a small square with a green plus sign) located to the right of Controller: SATA.

4
Select Your VDI File
Click Add in the Hard Disk Selector window, navigate to where you saved 24.04_VB_LinuxVMImages.COM.vdi, select it, and click Open. Then click Choose to finalize the selection.

5
Save and Start
Click OK to close the settings, then click the green Start arrow to boot the VM.

To verify this worked, watch the boot process. Instead of prompting you to install Ubuntu, the VM will boot directly to the Ubuntu login screen or desktop.

Cannot register the hard disk  because a hard disk with UUID already exists.

This error happens because you previously tried to attach or open this virtual disk while it was still inside a temporary extraction folder (AppData\Local\Temp\). VirtualBox remembered its unique internal ID (UUID), so it is blocking you from attaching the newly extracted copy located in your Downloads folder.

You need to tell VirtualBox to forget the old temporary file using the Virtual Media Manager.

1
Open Virtual Media Manager
In the main VirtualBox Manager window, click on File in the top menu, then select Tools, and click on Virtual Media Manager.

2
Locate the Conflicting Disk
Under the Hard disks tab, look through the list for Ubuntu_24.04_VB_LinuxVMImages.COM.vdi. Look at the "Location" path at the bottom of the window to find the one pointing to your AppData\Local\Temp folder.

3
Remove the Old Disk
Click on that temporary .vdi file to select it. Click the Release button at the top (if it is clickable), and then click the Remove button. When prompted, confirm the removal.

4
Attach the Correct Disk
Close the Virtual Media Manager. Go back to your VM's Settings > Storage, click the Add Hard Disk icon under your SATA controller, and select the .vdi file located in your Downloads folder.

To verify this worked, the error pop-up will not appear when you select the file, and Ubuntu_24.04_VB_LinuxVMImages.COM.vdi will successfully appear under the Controller in your Storage settings.
